<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Paul I of Russia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1754">1754</a>–<a href="/years/1801">1801</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Paul I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catherine-the-great">Catherine the Great</a>, reigned as emperor from 1796 to 1801. In 1799 he granted a monopoly charter to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-american-company">Russian-American Company</a> to govern Russian America, and in 1800 he decreed the annexation of the Georgian kingdom of Kartli-Kakheti, completed after his death. Erratic and autocratic, he was murdered by conspirators in 1801.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/paul-i-of-russia.jpg" alt="Paul I of Russia" width="320" height="437" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Vladimir Borovikovsky · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Emperor_Paul_I_of_Russia.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Paul was the son of Peter III and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catherine-the-great">Catherine II</a>, who was born Sophie of Anhalt-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zerbst">Zerbst</a>. Catherine hinted in one edition of her memoirs that her lover Sergei Saltykov was Paul's father, but later recanted and named Peter III. Empress Elizabeth took the infant almost at once, and he had limited contact with his mother. He was put in the charge of the governor Nikita Ivanovich Panin. He became crown prince in 1762, when his father took the throne, but within months Catherine deposed Peter III and Paul remained heir apparent. Mother and son kept a distant relationship. Paul criticised her expansionist wars in a dissertation on military reform, Reflections, which added to her suspicion of a conspiracy around him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Catherine suffered a stroke on 17 November 1796 and died without regaining consciousness. Paul's first act as emperor was to look for her testament and, if possible, destroy it, since he feared it would exclude him in favour of his son Alexander. He then promulgated the Pauline Laws, establishing strict male <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/primogeniture">primogeniture</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-romanov">House of Romanov</a>, rules that lasted to the end of the dynasty. He also imposed the first limits on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serfdom">serfdom</a> with the Manifesto of three-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corvee-labour">corvée</a>. He reversed many of his mother's policies, freed Alexander Radishchev, Nikolay Novikov and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tadeusz-kosciuszko">Tadeusz Kościuszko</a>, and sought to curtail the privileges of the nobility.</p><p>His army reforms, including Prussian uniforms impractical for active duty, were deeply unpopular. In foreign policy he recalled the troops Catherine had sent towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>. In 1799 he joined the Second Coalition against Revolutionary France with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, but withdrew after setbacks, and later led the Second League of Armed Neutrality against Britain. After the death of King Erekle of Kartli-Kakheti, civil war broke out over the succession, and one claimant appealed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>; Paul signed a decree incorporating the kingdom into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>. He was also Grand Master of the Russian Knights Hospitaller from 1799 to 1801.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Paul's attempts to impose a code of chivalry on the nobility alienated many advisers. A conspiracy organised by Peter Ludwig von der Pahlen, Nikita Petrovich Panin and José de Ribas was delayed by de Ribas's death in December 1800. On the night of 23 March 1801 (11 March in the old style) dismissed officers entered his bedroom in the newly completed Saint Michael's Castle, tried to force him to sign an abdication, and strangled and trampled him to death after Nikolay Zubov struck him with a sword. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-i-of-russia">Alexander I</a> succeeded him. There is some evidence that Paul was venerated as a saint by Russian Orthodox people, though no Orthodox Church canonised him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Paul I of Russia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>When George died in 1800, <b>Emperor Paul I</b> and then Alexander I annexed the kingdom in 1801, deposing the Bagratid dynasty and making it a Russian province.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-kartli-kakheti">Kingdom of Kartli-Kakheti</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1799 <b>Emperor Paul I</b> granted a monopoly charter to the Russian-American Company, which was to govern the territory on the state's behalf.</blockquote><a href="/state/russian-america">Russian America</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-petersburg">Saint Petersburg</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-petersburg">Saint Petersburg</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=59.95,30.32&amp;year=1784" aria-label="Saint Petersburg on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5185 154 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Paul I of Russia"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5635 424h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5635 424h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=59.95,30.32&amp;year=1784">What was here in 1784? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
